  "ai_language_tip": "Use polite Egyptian Arabic (Masri); Modern Standard sounds stiff but is understood; English works at hotels, tourist businesses and upscale Cairo/Alexandria places only.",
  "english_ok": "sometimes — Fine at hotels, tour operators and upscale restaurants and clinics in Cairo, Giza, Alexandria and Red Sea resorts; rare at local shops, workshops and government lines.",
  "politeness": "Warm and chatty: 'Salamu alaykum' or 'Masa' el-kheir', address men as 'ya fandem'/'Ustaz' and women as 'Madam'/'Ustaza', doctors as 'Doktor'; say you call on behalf of someone; close with 'shukran'.",
  "name_tip": "Bookings use first name, sometimes with father's name; clinics ask the patient's full name and age; spell for English systems.",
  "phone_tip": "Mobile 11 digits starting 010, 011, 012 or 015 (e.g. 010 1234 5678; +20 drops the 0); landline Cairo 02 plus 8 digits; WhatsApp on the same mobile is the norm.",
  "time_tip": "12-hour clock with 'el-sobh/el-dohr/el-'asr/bel-leil'; 'w nus' = :30, 'w rob'' = :15, 'ella rob'' = :45, 'w telt' = :20; evening life starts late, so '10' usually means 22:00 — always read back with day part.",
  "number_tip": "Arabic-Indic digits (٠–٩) and Western digits both appear; prices in pounds ('geneih'); Egyptians read phone numbers in small groups — confirm digit by digit.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid Friday noon prayer (about 11:30–13:30), early mornings before 10:00 (most shops open late), the iftar hour in Ramadan, and very late night calls even though shops stay open late.",
  "closed_days": "Government and many offices close Friday and Saturday; shops mostly open daily with longer hours on Thursday, Friday and holidays under the official summer/winter shop-hours decree; Eid holidays and national days close offices.",
  "booking_channels": "Doctors via Vezeeta or the clinic assistant; food via Talabat, elmenus, Breadfast and restaurant hotlines; rides via Uber, Careem, inDrive; shops and salons via Facebook pages, Instagram and WhatsApp; payments by cash, InstaPay and mobile wallets.",
  "voice_menu": "Telecoms, banks, chain restaurant hotlines (short codes) and government lines use Arabic IVR; small clinics, shops and workshops answer in person.",
  "callback_habits": "Clinics confirm queue by phone or WhatsApp; delivery drivers call for directions; small businesses reply on WhatsApp or call back from a personal mobile.",
